## Step 1 -- Set up the Terraform project and AWS provider

Marcus starts a new Terraform working directory for Haverbrook Tickets' infrastructure, pinning the AWS provider version so the whole team gets reproducible plans:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}
```

## Step 2 -- Provision the VPC

The VPC uses the community `terraform-aws-modules/vpc` module, with public subnets for the NAT gateway and private subnets for everything that shouldn't be internet-reachable -- the EKS nodes and the RDS instance:

```hcl
module "vpc" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws"
  version = "~> 5.0"

  name = "haverbrook-tickets-vpc"
  cidr = "10.20.0.0/16"

  azs             = ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b", "us-east-1c"]
  private_subnets = ["10.20.1.0/24", "10.20.2.0/24", "10.20.3.0/24"]
  public_subnets  = ["10.20.101.0/24", "10.20.102.0/24", "10.20.103.0/24"]

  enable_nat_gateway = true
  single_nat_gateway = true

  tags = {
    Project = "haverbrook-tickets"
  }
}
```

## Step 3 -- Provision the EKS cluster

The EKS cluster is built on top of the VPC's private subnets, using the `terraform-aws-modules/eks` module, with a managed node group sized as a reasonable starting point:

```hcl
module "eks" {
  source  = "terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws"
  version = "~> 20.0"

  cluster_name    = "haverbrook-tickets"
  cluster_version = "1.29"

  vpc_id     = module.vpc.vpc_id
  subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets

  eks_managed_node_groups = {
    default = {
      min_size       = 2
      max_size       = 6
      desired_size   = 3
      instance_types = ["t3.large"]
    }
  }

  tags = {
    Project = "haverbrook-tickets"
  }
}
```

The `dev`, `staging`, and `prod` namespaces planned in the Kubernetes planning lesson are created inside this one cluster once `kubectl`/Kubernetes-provider access is wired up in a later lesson -- this step provisions the cluster itself, not the namespaces.

## Step 4 -- Provision the ECR repositories

One repository per service, matching the CI/CD plan's push targets, each with scan-on-push enabled as a standing second check alongside the pipeline's own Trivy gate:

```hcl
resource "aws_ecr_repository" "catalog_api" {
  name                 = "catalog-api"
  image_tag_mutability = "IMMUTABLE"

  image_scanning_configuration {
    scan_on_push = true
  }
}

resource "aws_ecr_repository" "checkout_api" {
  name                 = "checkout-api"
  image_tag_mutability = "IMMUTABLE"

  image_scanning_configuration {
    scan_on_push = true
  }
}
```

`image_tag_mutability = "IMMUTABLE"` enforces, at the registry level, the git-SHA tagging convention planned in the CI/CD lesson -- once a tag is pushed, it cannot be overwritten.

## Step 5 -- Provision the RDS Postgres instance

The database sits in the VPC's private subnets only, reachable solely from the EKS node security group:

```hcl
resource "aws_db_subnet_group" "haverbrook" {
  name       = "haverbrook-tickets-db"
  subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets
}

resource "aws_security_group" "rds" {
  name   = "haverbrook-tickets-rds"
  vpc_id = module.vpc.vpc_id

  ingress {
    from_port       = 5432
    to_port         = 5432
    protocol        = "tcp"
    security_groups = [module.eks.node_security_group_id]
  }
}

resource "aws_db_instance" "haverbrook" {
  identifier              = "haverbrook-tickets-db"
  engine                  = "postgres"
  engine_version          = "16.3"
  instance_class          = "db.t3.medium"
  allocated_storage       = 50
  db_subnet_group_name    = aws_db_subnet_group.haverbrook.name
  vpc_security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.rds.id]
  db_name                 = "haverbrook"
  username                = "haverbrook_app"
  manage_master_user_password = true
  skip_final_snapshot     = false
}
```

`manage_master_user_password = true` lets AWS Secrets Manager generate and own the master password, rather than Terraform state holding it in plaintext.

## Step 6 -- Plan, review, and apply

Marcus runs `terraform plan`, reviews every resource it proposes to create against this plan, and applies it. With the VPC, EKS cluster, both ECR repositories, and the RDS instance provisioned, the infrastructure the CI/CD pipeline will push images to and the Kubernetes manifests will deploy into finally exists. Containerizing catalog-api and checkout-api, and wiring the CI/CD pipeline planned earlier against this real infrastructure, are the implementation lessons immediately ahead.
